  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "Are mutual exclusivity and independence the same thing?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "No. Mutually exclusive means the events cannot occur simultaneously; independent means whether one occurs does not affect whether the other occurs",
    "B": "Yes, the two terms mean exactly the same thing",
    "C": "Yes, mutually exclusive events must be independent",
    "D": "No, independent means the two events have equal probabilities"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "When both probabilities are positive, mutually exclusive events cannot be independent, because once one occurs, the conditional probability of the other becomes \\(0\\).",
